  about: 'Almost every hymn in the Rig Veda is addressed to a deity — the poet speaks, the god is spoken to. This one inverts everything. Here the goddess speaks, in her own voice, for eight verses, and she does not wait to be praised. She is Vāk — Speech, the Word itself — and she announces her own power with a confidence that has no parallel in the collection: "I travel with the gods… I hold up Mitra and Varuna… the man I love, I make mighty… I have entered earth and heaven… I breathe like the wind, holding all existence together." It is the earliest sustained first-person voice of the divine feminine in world literature, and its subject is the most quietly astonishing choice imaginable: not a mother goddess, not a warrior, but Language — the power of the word, declaring that everything that is seen, breathed, and heard happens through her. Three thousand years before anyone argued that we live inside language, a Vedic poet had Speech herself say so.',
