      commentary: 'The hymn ends not with a summary but with an expansion, the voice widening until it exceeds the frame of everything. She compares herself to the wind — vāta iva — invisible, everywhere, touching all it passes, taking hold of "all the worlds." And the final line simply keeps growing: beyond the heaven, beyond the earth, "so vast have I become in my greatness." There is no resolution, no return to the human worshipper, no closing petition. Speech, having declared herself the medium of the gods, the ground of perception, the maker of sages, and the womb in the cosmic waters, ends by outgrowing the cosmos itself. It is the right ending for this hymn, because it enacts what it describes: language, once it begins, exceeds every boundary you try to set for it. The last word is mahinā, "in greatness" — and the sentence, fittingly, does not so much conclude as trail off into the vastness it is naming. Later tradition would gather this goddess into Saraswati and into the Devi of the great goddess hymns; but here, at the source, she is simpler and stranger than any of them — she is the Word, awake and speaking, and she has just told you that you have been living inside her all along.'
